course: dictionary
lesson: 13291
title: Gramps and other family words
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words are informal. People use them with friends and family, in speech and in chatty writing, but not in schoolwork or formal letters. «Gramps» is one of these words, and it is a friendly name for your grandad. «My gramps taught me to play chess when I was six.» Here gramps means grandfather, and the warm feeling in the sentence shows it is a family word.
explain: Context tells you where a word is used. «Gramps came over from Canada to see us.» Canada is a clue: gramps is a word people use a lot in the USA, Canada and Australia. In the UK, people more often say «Grandad» or «Grandpa». Different places have different words for the same person, and that is normal.
explain: Context can also show when a word is rude. «The boy at the bus stop shouted, "Oi, gramps, move your bag!" The old man looked hurt.» The boy was not talking about his own grandad. He was using gramps as a cheeky way of talking to a stranger who was old. So gramps can mean grandfather, but it can also sound rude, so think about who you are talking to.
fact: «Gramps» is an informal noun, so it belongs in chatty speech and friendly writing.
fact: «Gramps» means grandfather, especially as a friendly name for your own grandad.
fact: «Gramps» is used most in the USA, Canada and Australia; in the UK people more often say «Grandad» or «Grandpa».
fact: Used about a stranger, «gramps» can be cheeky or rude, so think about who you are talking to.
fact: A good check is to ask who the word is for: a family member, or a stranger?
exercise: «My gramps taught me to ride a bike.» What does gramps mean here? | A type of bicycle | A school teacher | *Grandfather | A next-door neighbour
exercise: A boy calls an old man at the bus stop «gramps» and the man looks hurt. Why? | It means the man is the boy's grandad | It means the man is very rich | *It can sound rude when used about a stranger | It is a kind word for a teacher
exercise: In the UK, which word do people more often use for grandfather? | *Grandad | Granny | Uncle | Gramps
test: «Gramps» is an informal word. Which meaning is the friendly family one? | Dangerous | Hungry | Very quiet | *Grandfather
test: Why might you not use «gramps» in a formal letter to your head teacher? | Because it means a stranger | *Because it is informal, so it fits chatty speech and friendly writing | Because it is a word for a type of car | Because it is too long
exam: Which sentence uses gramps in the cheeky way, not as a family name? | «My gramps made pancakes for us.» | «Gramps lives in Sydney with Nana.» | «We visited gramps every Sunday.» | *«Oi, gramps, move your bag!» said the boy to the stranger.
